SM(531,{"n":"1 Corinthians 3-4","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Corinthians 3-4.mp3","d":1991.9,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.96,"So we'll go ahead and get started."],[22.8,"When we get started in just a minute, we'll be picking up, stop sort of in the middle of a chapter, so we'll be picking up in St."],[29.28,"Paul's first epistle to the Corinthians chapter three, verse eighteen, which is where we left off last time."],[36.56,"Just to sort of quickly review."],[48.56,"We've already been tipped off to some of them, even in these first couple of chapters."],[53.12,"The first one that St."],[54.4,"Paul has been dealing with has been that the sort of factions had arisen where different people had formed sort of fan clubs of different apostles."],[63.32,"Some people were saying that they were, oh, we're Pauline, and then other people were saying they're Petrine, St."],[71.08,"Peter or Apollos or someone else."],[74.28,"And Saint Paul has been very quick and deliberate to refute that."],[78.92,"He's pointed out that it's only Christ who died for them, it's only Christ they should follow, and that they as Christians have the opportunity to build on the foundation that Saint Paul laid in their life in order to produce something beautiful and something that will uh last forever."],[96.52,"And he's been doing sort of a comparative."],[99.0,"The other major theme that he's introduced in these first couple of chapters, uh, that we're definitely going to get more into later is he's already been, by playing with the vocabulary, been talking about paganism that Corinth was, of course, rife with."],[114.5,"The Roman religion, with the mysteries of Demeter, and how the pagans sort of seek wisdom through initiation into these mysteries and through this pagan worship of idols and and pagan gods, and uh that there's nothing there's nothing there for them, that there's no wisdom there for them, that all the true wisdom is in Christ."],[137.3,"They already have Christ because they've received the Holy Spirit."],[140.9,"So running after these things is running after ignorance."],[145.16,"That what's presenting itself to them as wisdom out in the world is actually ignorance."],[149.16,"And what they may look at when they look at St"],[154.56,"Paul and when they look at Christ on the cross, and when they look at the other apostles, they might see that as weakness and foolishness."],[163.6,"In reality, that's where the true wisdom lies."],[167.84,"So that's a that's a quick catch up."],[172.16,"Are there any questions left over or comments?"],[175.44,"We can plunge ahead."],[176.72,"Okay. We'll go ahead then and get started here in chapter 3, verse 18, and that's exactly where Saint Paul picks up."],[186.4,"Let no one deceive himself."],[188.32,"If anyone among you seems to be wise in this age, let him become a fool that he may become wise."],[194.76,"So that on its face seems sort of paradoxical, but it's in keeping with what he's been saying."],[200.2,"Those of you who think you're wise because you have all this wisdom that's come from."],[205.4,"And remember, when he says world, he doesn't there is no concept of secular for Saint Paul or anyone else living in the first century."],[214.2,"That concept doesn't exist."],[215.72,"So he's not talking about when he talks about the world, he's not talking about some kind of secular realm or book learning or any of those things."],[226.04,"Uh, he's talking about the wisdom of the world is specifically religious wisdom"],[231.64,"So this is coming either from paganism or from philosophical sources."],[236.2,"And those philosophical sources, again aren't secular."],[238.84,"The Stoics, it's not that the Stoics were atheists who had a philosophy"],[245.47,"Their philosophical view of the world was based on what we would today call their religious beliefs about who God was and all of these and all these things."],[256.11,"So he's specifically talking about that."],[258.35,"For example, this gets applied to the idea that somehow academic learning or intellectualism is somehow quote unquote worldly."],[267.79,"Um, and that's not at all what he's talking about."],[270.51,"St. Paul is definitely not an anti-intellectual."],[273.31,"We've only been through an epistle in a couple of chapters, but I think it's pretty obvious to everyone that he is not."],[280.15,"He's not an anti-intellectual, he is definitely not dumb."],[283.11,"Well, even the way he says is according to classical rhetoric."],[289.13,"So so the person who thinks himself wise because he has this kind of knowledge from out in the pagan world, uh, needs to become a fool, needs to divest himself of that, needs to become like St."],[303.69,"Paul, like the apostles, like Christ on the cross, so that he can get the true wisdom, and become wise."],[312.43,"He needs to start over."],[313.71,"Would St. Paul say that the that classical um philosophical wisdom is incompatible with the wisdom of Christ?"],[323.39,"Because there were later people who thought they were very compatible."],[326.67,"Yeah, there were both sides."],[327.47,"Right Yeah. And so this is this is part of what I was getting at with the idea that that St."],[337.97,"Paul is not anti-reason."],[340.05,"He's anti-paganism. So all the places where these beliefs are grounded in these pagan gods, who he believes, as we saw, are real entities."],[353.03,"He believes they're demons."],[354.07,"He doesn't think they don't exist."],[355.77,"He thinks these are demonic spirits who are misleading people."],[360.09,"And we talked about, uh I think at least we mentioned last time that Socrates twice, once in the Republic and once in uh The Apology."],[369.21,"Those are the unambiguous ones. talks about being possessed by a demon as a good thing"],[375.25,"So those philosophers would categorize their knowledge as demonic."],[380.93,"They would mean something different."],[382.39,"They would mean it was inspired by the gods and was somehow divine."],[386.79,"But St. Paul doesn't come and argue on a secular basis."],[391.11,"No, this is just your theory and your ideas."],[394.39,"He agrees that the phenomenon is coming from the spiritual realm."],[398.23,"He just disagrees about what kind of spirit that is and how truthful it is."],[402.23,"So I don't want to give the idea that St"],[406.15,"Paul thought you could sort of separate the two."],[409.11,"Because again, that's the idea of the secular."],[411.51,"Like that you could sort of filter through Plato and say, okay, well, here here''s thes the non-pagan stuff and then pagan stuff."],[420.27,"I don't think you can say that from St."],[422.75,"Paul. What St."],[423.55,"Paul uses, like, for example, in Acts 17, when he quotes a hymn to Zeus, when he uses these things, he's using them kind of deliberately as a bait and switch."],[433.79,"He's using them as an evangelism tactic."],[436.27,"Here's an argument that I know you will agree with because it's coming from your, you know, as he says in Acts 17, what have your own poets said?"],[444.65,"So he's using that as something they know, but then he takes it through his argument and turns it on its head."],[450.49,"He says, this isn't that the God who this is true of isn't Zeus."],[455.9,"It's this God who I'm here proclaiming."],[458.0,"And so he turns those things around."],[458.96,"So yeah, it's and and part of it"],[464.52,"I'm concerned about going too far down a rabbit trail, but I want to answer the question, and this isn't this is an important issue, especially here where St."],[471.4,"Paul's talking about it."],[472.76,"The understanding of reason in the ancient world is very different than our understanding of reason."],[478.36,"Our understanding of reason is grounded in math, and the laws of logic."],[485.24,"Which again are things that we think of as secular."],[488.06,"We think of these things as just available to everyone."],[491.82,"And they didn't really have that concept of reason in the ancient world."],[497.26,"In the ancient world, the words for reason and wisdom were. they saw the reason, the noose, which is the word that's not just an orthodox theology word"],[509.14,"That's the word that Plato and Aristotle use."],[513.22,"For reason. For reason."],[514.98,"That's that's the it's the high for Aristotle, the Nicobackeean ethics, that's the highest of the uh rational virtues is the noose."],[524.02,"And they see reason and the intellect as a sensory organ, not as sort of we do."],[532.98,"We we tend to see our brain as like a computer in a box that processes information."],[538.67,"And so thoughts are generated by our brain."],[542.59,"That's not all the way they saw it in the ancient world."],[545.31,"So in the ancient world, your mind is sort of like another sense."],[547.47,"And it receives thoughts"],[554.28,"So thoughts come into your mind from outside."],[557.96,"Uh-huh. The mind is just perceiving them like eyes perceived."],[562.82,"Pagans, Jews, Christians all uh all believed this."],[567.32,"So this is why when when the pagan went out into the woods at night with their romantic partner and were inspired with feelings of love andor lust, they interpreted that as the spirits in the woods giving them these thoughts."],[587.08,"The closest thing we've got in our modern culture would be like Cupid popping up with his little arrow and shooting somebody and then they fall in love."],[591.16,"Which is sort of a hackney version of it"],[596.64,"But but they believed that right that thoughts are come into your head from the spiritual world."],[605.12,"There's a sort of fossil of this in our language because we speak of sense meaning as in making sense."],[613.61,"And that's really referring to reason."],[616.42,"As a sense."],[617.48,"And so the honing of your reason right like Aristotle is a philosophical virtue."],[622.32,"The idea of the honing of your reason is the idea that you're able to perceive more correctly and more adequately these things."],[630.76,"So, you know, the ancient people thought that what we call reason, they called it something more like calculation."],[634.36,"called it Aristotle the Loisticon, for which we get logistics in English."],[637.2,"Uh-huh. Logistics is okay, we've had the big idea, logistics is okay, now we figure out all the little details, you know, to make it happen."],[654.42,"That was this lesser thing, what we call reason now, was this lesser thing in the ancient world."],[659.94,"And real reason was being able to perceive these things mentally."],[664.66,"And and this factors into this is why it's very difficult from a modern materialist point of view to understand how uh contemplative prayer works, especially in the Eastern tradition and the Jesus prayer, because we think that sort of the stream of consciousness in our head, all those thoughts, is us."],[688.3,"Like we're the subject who's producing those."],[690.62,"And of course, in the ancient view, that's not true."],[694.36,"That's not us."],[696.04,"And so uh what contemplative prayer and what the Jesus prayer is aimed at, the Jesus that's why the Jesus prayer is referred to as uh the prayer of a single thought."],[707.26,"It's a prayer discipline where we take all those thoughts that are coming in from all over the place and that have us confused and running all over, we sort of silence those by focusing on only one thought."],[719.83,"So there's only one thought in there, and by focusing on only one thought, that allows us to ex sort of extract ourselves from that stream of consciousness and separate ourselves from it."],[731.47,"And eventually, people, not me, much holier people than me, get to the point where they can even silence that one thought and just sort of in a pure way contemplate God."],[744.19,"And so that's yet another level."],[746.83,"But being able to abstract yourself from that stream of consciousness is what allows us to then look at our thoughts and examine our thoughts because we're not sort of immersed in them."],[757.09,"Like if you're in a river getting pulled downstream, you can't really take a look at the river."],[763.65,"Look at the banks, look at the surroundings, figure out how wide and how deep the river is."],[768.15,"Because you're the right that's you're you're in the midst of it."],[771.19,"So it's only when you get outside of it that you can start examining your thoughts and saying, where are these coming from?"],[777.19,"Which are good, which are evil."],[779.13,"Which do I need to get rid of, and which should I cling to?"],[782.91,"And so that's why that's why that uh discipline is important."],[788.32,"So when St."],[790.72,"Paul would have looked at Plato or one of the Platonists, or another philosopher, he's not treating reason as, well, here's their logic."],[802.18,"Okay, point A, point B, point C, where do they go wrong?"],[806.74,"What of this can I keep?"],[808.52,"He's looking at the way that they perce.ived, particularly the spiritual realm"],[814.72,"And so places where they had accurate perceptions he could use to proclaim the gospel."],[823.28,"And then places where they had inaccurate or lying or other perceptions were places that he needed to counter."],[828.8,"So the the wisdom of this world that he's talking about, this pagan wisdom, whether it's remember, there's no division between philosophical and religious."],[838.58,"At this time."],[839.32,"So whether it's coming from what we would call like Stoicism, epicureanism, or pay what we would call paganism, because there wasn't even a word for that at the time."],[850.52,"From paganism, either way, that's what he's looking at."],[853.88,"And what he's talking about is their perceptions of the spiritual realm, which are, of course, if they're pagan, off base."],[864.4,"Uh-huh. They're a misperception."],[867.13,"Not a misperception in the sense that they're imaginary."],[871.31,"But or they're hallucinating."],[873.63,"But in the sense that they've been lied to, by these beings who they've interacted with."],[879.57,"Because when we get a few more chapters ahead, when he starts talking about pagan sacrifices, he's going to affirm very strongly that they are really interacting with demons."],[889.89,"Just like they say they are."],[891.75,"He's saying, But you as Christians need to not be right, not be doing that."],[896.25,"Because these spirits are not communicating truth to them."],[896.73,"spirits are not communicating. These to them in a way that's going to help them"],[903.25,"It's they're working to bring about their destruction."],[906.14,"And the destruction of God's creation and chaos and and all these things."],[910.94,"So that's what he's talking about."],[912.86,"But the person in the culture at Corinth, who is the most quote unquote spiritually advanced, the one who's an oracle who's being possessed, you know, or the one like Socrates, who has this demon living within him, that person is looked at by the culture."],[929.02,"Oh, this is the person who is most spiritual, most wise, closest to the gods."],[934.7,"And so if I have a not only them putting on airs, but if I have a problem, if I have a question, if I need I go to this person."],[941.98,"this person I go to wise to get their wisdom."],[942.38,"And St I go to the oracle at Delphi, to go and find out the future."],[949.46,"Paul's saying, uh uh."],[952.16,"Because the beings they're communicating, as we talked about last time, the beings they're communicating with don't know anything anyway."],[958.16,"And because Christ is God and the Holy Spirit is God, you have the Holy Spirit dwelling within you, connecting you to Christ."],[964.58,"Christ is the truth, you have access to all truth in that way."],[969.78,"To go back to your original question, we see everything Plato that wrote as the product of his human mind."],[978.07,"And then even today, most scholars, especially, but even non-scholars, even people who are dedicated Christians talk about St."],[985.11,"Paul's mind Paul's epistles as if they're the product of St."],[992.35,"Yes. That he sat and he thought about religion."],[996.03,"Or he thought about Christ and then wrote these things."],[999.55,"But that's not how Saint Paul or Plato understood what they were doing at all."],[1006.19,"So St. Paul, from St."],[1009.15,"Paul's self-understanding, is writing about his own spiritual experience, which was as real to him as his physical experience."],[1017.87,"He's writing about what Christ has taught him."],[1021.17,"And he says that in various places in his letters."],[1023.87,"And as we saw, Christ appeared to him not only once, but at least three times in the book of Acts and spoke to him."],[1032.51,"So when he talks about having a relationship with Christ, that's literal."],[1036.91,"That's not just he has some sort of sense."],[1040.23,"That's and so, yeah, so he's writing about his spiritual experience."],[1043.99,"And Plato, from that testimony through the person of Socrates, thinks the same thing."],[1049.13,"Aristotle, when he writes his metaphysics, is talking about his experience of being and of the greatest being in an almost prayerful way."],[1058.53,"ay right W when you read it, he's not just doing conjecture."],[1062.75,"And so, yeah, we have to we can't address it on those terms."],[1067.07,"Like, what would St."],[1068.11,"Paul think was valid out of Plato's writings?"],[1070.35,"Well, he wouldn't look at Plato's writings that way."],[1073.51,"So if you mean by value valid, true, he would say, yeah, this reflects their experience, but their experience was with lying spirits."],[1086.15,"And with so, yeah, so the it's a different view of uh what reason is, what truth is, and what wisdom is here that sort of produces different results."],[1099.43,"Yeah, it that that resonates in lots of places."],[1103.67,"Yeah, so yeah, but it it is important, and it it's it's one of the fundamental issues with interpreting the scriptures in the modern world, is that uh I know I've probably said this many times, but we look at the past and we project ourselves into it."],[1123.73,"So we assume that St."],[1126.05,"Paul's understanding of Christianity was basically like mine, his religious experience was like mine, that he, you know, saw the world the same way I do."],[1138.23,"And of course, as soon as I say that, you say, Well, obviously that's not true."],[1142.08,"He lived 2,000 years ago in a completely different culture and a completely of course he didn't."],[1146.8,"But we do it anyway."],[1148.0,"But we assume it."],[1149.2,"We assume it."],[1150.0,"And we assume it by the way we read and interpret the scriptures a lot, because we're reading and interpreting them in modern English, you know, or Arabic."],[1160.24,"In another culture in another place in another time right and so we read them and interpret them through our frame rather than trying to say well how can we and we have in our age we have plenty of information to reconstruct the worldview of the the ancient worldview of the time and and try and read it from that worldview but even before our era now you know academically we have all these ancient texts and we have them translated and you say well in the 13th century and in Constantinople they, didn't have that."],[1193.06,"No, but they had the liturgy of the church."],[1196.02,"And the liturgy of the church is still coming from that perspective."],[1201.3,"And so that's where the idea of interpreting the scriptures in the church comes from."],[1209.14,"Because the church has maintained that ancient perspective and its practices."],[1214.66,"For example, the prayer practices I was just talking about."],[1218.12,"That have sort of maintained this sense of what reason is from the ancient world, even though culturally we look at it very differently."],[1227.32,"So all that in response to the first verse."],[1230.52,"But no, but it is important."],[1231.96,"It is important for everything, everything we talk about in this Bible study."],[1236.44,"So verse 19 for the wisdom of this world is foolishness with God, for it is written, he catches the wise in their own craftiness."],[1244.2,"And again, the Lord knows the thoughts of the wise that they are futile."],[1248.44,"So who are the wise here that he's talking about?"],[1252.72,"The people who have a lot of the knowledge that's really from the pagan gods."],[1259.41,"And the rulers of this world, those gods themselves."],[1264.69,"So, for example, that uh craftiness."],[1267.73,"Do you remember who was described as being awfully clever in the book of Genesis?"],[1273.57,"Tip off. The serpent."],[1276.13,"Oh, was more clever and more subtle than all the than all the other the other creatures."],[1283.97,"Uh-huh. And so St."],[1286.13,"Paul isn't firmly distinguishing between the two."],[1290.35,"What he's saying is true of both of them."],[1293.11,"As craftiness does in English, an air of sneakiness to it."],[1297.28,"That these spirits are manipulating us."],[1299.84,"They think they're being crafty."],[1302.16,"And this and St."],[1303.2,"Paul is here referring to both the spiritual enemies of God and those who follow them."],[1310.9,"Think that they're wise, think that they're clever."],[1313.7,"But God knows their thoughts."],[1317.04,"That they're futile, meaning they'll come to nothing."],[1319.36,"Which is exactly remember what St."],[1320.96,"Paul just said about the rulers of this world."],[1323.12,"They're coming to nothing."],[1324.5,"It's already over for them."],[1326.26,"Therefore, let no one boast in men, for all things are yours."],[1330.68,"That's that emphasis."],[1332.12,"All things are yours already, because you have the Holy Spirit."],[1336.28,"You just need to come to maturity in your understanding"],[1336.88,"So you've got it all at least potentially."],[1340.0,"Uh-huh. Even though these people in Corinth are doing a very bad job of being Christians, they do have the spirit."],[1350.08,"And remember St."],[1350.96,"Paul just compared them to babies."],[1352.88,"Yes. He said spiritually you're babies."],[1355.81,"A baby is human in our modern terms."],[1358.13,"They've already got all their DNA."],[1359.89,"Uh-huh. Everything is there potentially for them to become a full-grown adult human."],[1366.23,"It's all there in potency."],[1368.53,"But they need to bring that to actuality, to reality."],[1373.33,"Very aristotelian. Yes."],[1375.25,"Exactly. And they're not doing that."],[1377.09,"Uh-huh. They're still babies."],[1379.19,"Uh-huh. And they have this infantile sort of understanding of things"],[1384.67,"He says, whether Paul or Apollos or Cephas or the world or life or death or things present to come, or present or things to come, all are yours."],[1395.47,"And you are Christ's, and Christ is God's."],[1409.83,"Or Apollos or Peter or the world or life."],[1415.81,"Or something going on out there in the world, or something that's going to happen in the future."],[1419.25,"There's nothing that they have to teach you."],[1422.85,"And where is he getting that idea?"],[1424.93,"He's drawing on this is this is straight out of the Old Testament tradition."],[1429.41,"What's the Holy Spirit going to do when he comes, according to Jeremiah 31, and Joel 2 and Ezekiel when it talks about the new covenant, he's going to come and he's going to write the Torah upon your heart."],[1446.1,"And no more, no longer does it say, will a man have to teach his brother to know the Lord?"],[1453.38,"Because everyone will."],[1455.06,"So he's saying, I'm a I'm irrelevant to boot."],[1460.26,"I happen to be the person who came there to told yourself originally, but you now have everything."],[1464.74,"You have everything because you have the Holy Spirit."],[1467.26,"Because you have the Holy Spirit, you belong to Christ."],[1468.34,"belong Christs to the Father."],[1468.74,"So he's being Trinitarian about it"],[1474.92,"All of God is in you and is available to you."],[1478.36,"You're just not doing it."],[1481.0,"Uh-huh. Not using it."],[1482.52,"Not using it."],[1483.48,"Not doing it."],[1484.2,"And so, and the reason you're not doing it is because you're looking for that wisdom here or there."],[1490.76,"You're looking for it in some teacher, some Christian teacher, or worse, some pagan teacher."],[1496.82,"Or somewhere out there in the world, that's not where you need to be looking."],[1500.6,"Because they don't have anything to offer you."],[1502.84,"So, chapter four, verse 1."],[1504.64,"Let a man so consider us as servants of Christ and stewards of the mysteries of God."],[1510.92,"So he says, This is this is how you should look at me."],[1512.52,"Peter and at Apollos"],[1514.16,"He's gone now for some time about how you shouldn't look at St."],[1517.04,"Paul, and how you shouldn't look at St."],[1519.84,"He says, So look at us as servants of Christ and stewards of the mysteries of God."],[1526.54,"He's using that mystery language again."],[1529.66,"So we're just the servants."],[1531.98,"We're the stewards in his house."],[1534.06,"We guide you in when you show up."],[1537.82,"We take your coat."],[1539.18,"Well, now, stewards of the mysteries of God sounds in in my modern years, like we've got the mysteries and we're going to pass them on to you, which is exactly what he's not saying."],[1551.63,"So what again, mysteries here, we've got to remember the context of Corinth."],[1556.03,"Mysteries isn't I know all the secrets of God and I'm going to tell them to you"],[1556.27,"Mysteries.y, you're initiated into myster"],[1564.63,"And so when you go to be initiated into the mysteries, you're trying to come into contact with the gods, with spirits directly."],[1574.23,"Okay. Now that involves there's a whole bunch of people who are sort of doing the ritual with you, uh, often wearing masks or in the dark, or so you can't see them."],[1584.29,"You're not coming to see them."],[1586.21,"You're not coming to the the you're coming to whoever the God is."],[1591.75,"So in this case, you're coming to God the Father through Christ."],[1595.99,"That's who you're coming to see."],[1597.77,"We're the helpers."],[1599.99,"And so this is how mysteries comes to in the Orthodox Church refer to the sacraments."],[1604.44,"When the West are called the sacraments."],[1606.6,"The obvious example here would be baptism."],[1609.56,"You didn't when you came to be baptized, you came to be baptized into Christ, as he's already pointed out, not to be baptized into Paul."],[1617.64,"And so I'm the steward there who pushed you under the water."],[1621.58,"I'm the guy who put but right, I'm not who you came to to see."],[1626.24,"I'm a functionary."],[1627.82,"That's what he's getting at."],[1629.66,"Moreover, it is required in stewards that one be found faithful."],[1633.42,"So he says, So then he says, This is how you ought to look at us, and then how should we be judged if that's who we are?"],[1642.46,"Because what they had been do beening doing would have if I'm in the St."],[1643.5,"Paul fan club and you're in the St"],[1648.5,"Peter fan club, well, then we're gonna have arguments about who's better."],[1652.84,"Well, yeah, but you know."],[1655.68,"He's like, so it number one, we're all servants."],[1659.28,"And number two, here's how you assess a servant, how faithful they are in serving their master."],[1665.84,"But with me, it is a very small thing that I should be judged by you or by a human court."],[1671.28,"In fact, I do not even judge myself."],[1674.82,"So he says, basically, I don't care."],[1678.56,"How you judge me."],[1679.88,"That is how you judge a servant, but I don't care what you think of me."],[1682.84,"I meingan both the mess."],[1684.12,"I don't care if you're my fan one of those who's not, who thinks I stink."],[1684.16,"I don't care if you're my fan."],[1685.48,"Who thinks I'm the worst apostle"],[1692.54,"Great. I'm the worst apostle."],[1694.62,"If that's what you think."],[1696.38,"For I know of nothing against myself, yet I am not justified by this, but he who judges me is the Lord."],[1703.36,"So he says, now I don't know of any place where I'm going wrong."],[1706.32,"Why? Well, because if he did, he'd repent of it."],[1709.68,"So he says, I'm not aware of anything, you know, that you could really accuse me of, but that's not what justified."],[1717.04,"Remember what we talked about justification at the beginning of Romans?"],[1720.32,"It's who's on Christ's right instead of his left, who's said to be righteous."],[1724.0,"He says, But the fact that I can't think of anything off the top of my head, any sins, I'm actively committing, that's not what makes me a good servant."],[1730.16,"That's not what makes me righteous"],[1736.44,"Judgment is the Lord's."],[1738.36,"Christ is going to judge me."],[1740.36,"And he'll decide."],[1741.56,"Yeah. The kind of job I've done and how faithful I've been."],[1745.64,"Therefore, judge nothing before the time until the Lord comes, who will both bring to light the hidden things of darkness and reveal the counsels of the hearts."],[1754.84,"Then each one's praise will come from God."],[1758.68,"He says, So it's not your job right now, to be looking at St."],[1765.17,"Paul or St."],[1766.05,"Peter or St."],[1767.57,"Apollos, or each other, or anyone else, and trying to decide who are the good people and who are the bad people."],[1774.69,"That's not your job"],[1774.99,"Who are the righteous and who are the sinners?"],[1777.15,"Who are the good Christians and who are the lousy Christians?"],[1781.55,"Christ is coming."],[1783.39,"He's going to judge."],[1785.55,"And by the way, when he comes to judge, he is going to both bring to light the things of darkness, referring to the spiritual evil."],[1794.59,"He's going to expose them, and what they've been doing."],[1797.93,"And he knows what's actually going on in your heart."],[1801.05,"And he's going to reveal that too, one way or another."],[1804.49,"So he's the one who's actually qualified to judge."],[1807.61,"He's going to do it."],[1809.13,"Until that happens, not our job."],[1812.17,"And then if anyone's praised."],[1814.43,"So he talks about praise because he says, Look, it's not just. I'm not saying this because, oh, you're judging me and I'm upset"],[1823.11,"Which is the way we hear don't judge a lot."],[1826.07,"In our modern world, is it's from people who are doing things they shouldn't be doing and don't like being told that."],[1832.49,"They say, don't judge me."],[1834.01,"That's how St."],[1834.81,"Paul's saying. St."],[1835.61,"Paul's saying, I don't want your praise."],[1837.61,"Uh-huh. Those of you who are fans of me who are going around saying I'm of Paul, I don't want your praise."],[1843.53,"I don't want you to be fans."],[1844.89,"I don't want that from you."],[1846.57,"Because that's a judgment, too."],[1848.17,"He says, I want you to not worry about me."],[1851.29,"And so the only praise he's concerned about getting will be if he receives praise from God on the day of judgment when he stands before Christ."],[1859.37,"If God praises him on that day for his the faithfulness he's shown in his life, he wants that."],[1863.05,"he's But like, I don't, I don't care what you folks in Corinth think of me."],[1871.05,"You can all hate me for all I care."],[1872.81,"But really, it would be better for you if you didn't judge at all."],[1878.83,"If you didn't spend so much time forming opinions of other people."],[1881.71,"That would kill gossip."],[1883.31,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah."],[1887.31,"Because that's what gossip basically is."],[1889.23,"Yeah. Now, these things, brethren, I have figuratively transferred to myself, and Apollos for your sakes, that you may learn in us not to think beyond what is written, that none of you may be puffed up on behalf of one against the other."],[1904.99,"So he says, Lo,ok I've been talking. about me and Apollos and Peter"],[1911.91,"But this applies to all of you as well, in terms of judging each other."],[1916.87,"Because as he points out, if you're the one who's doing the judging, it seems to always work out."],[1923.27,"It's very rare that you really want run into somebody."],[1926.47,"It does happen."],[1927.59,"But it's rather rare that you run into somebody who really thinks he's the worst of everybody."],[1932.15,"It's really rare that any of us are sincere when we say that Christ came into the world to save sinners of whom I am chief."],[1939.19,"Usually we've got a cousin or somebody somebody in the back of our head who we're like, well, okay, I'm not that bad."],[1945.88,"But and so what St."],[1948.16,"Paul is pointing out is that anytime we judge, invariably, we're gonna find somebody, and what we're gonna focus on is those somebodies who we think we're doing better."],[1960.28,"Which is what he's talking about when he says becoming puffed up, inflated."],[1965.06,"Having pride, which is going to destroy us."],[1969.54,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[1976.9,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[1982.26,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.8,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: factions and pagan wisdom","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>1 Corinthians 3:18</b>, middle of a chapter</li><li class=\"d0\">First problem: <b>factions</b> - \"fan clubs\" of apostles</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we're Pauline\" / \"we're Petrine\" / Apollos</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul quick to refute: only <b>Christ died for them</b>, only Christ to follow</li><li class=\"d1\">they <b>build on the foundation</b> Paul laid → something beautiful that <b>lasts forever</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Second theme: <b>paganism</b> - Corinth \"rife\" with it</li><li class=\"d1\">Roman religion, <b>mysteries of Demeter</b>, initiation, idols and gods</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul plays with the vocabulary of these mysteries</li><li class=\"d1\">\"nothing there for them\" - <b>all true wisdom is in Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they already have Christ: received the <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Running after these = running after <b>ignorance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what looks like wisdom out in the world is <b>ignorance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul, Christ on the cross, apostles look like weakness and foolishness - <b>that's where true wisdom lies</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: any questions left over? → plunge ahead</li></ul>"},{"t":176.72,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 3:18 - Become a fool to become wise","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:18</b>: \"Let no one deceive himself. If anyone among you seems to be wise in this age, let him become a <b>fool</b> that he may become wise\"</li><li class=\"d1\">on its face <b>paradoxical</b>, but in keeping with what he's been saying</li><li class=\"d0\">\"World\" - <b>no concept of secular</b> for Paul or anyone in the 1st century</li><li class=\"d1\">not talking about a secular realm or <b>book learning</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wisdom of the world = specifically <b>religious wisdom</b></li><li class=\"d1\">comes from <b>paganism or philosophical sources</b> - and those aren't secular either</li><li class=\"d1\">Stoics weren't atheists with a philosophy - their view of the world rested on <b>beliefs about who God was</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Misapplied: academic learning / intellectualism as \"worldly\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul is <b>definitely not anti-intellectual</b> - obvious from the epistle; he even uses <b>classical rhetoric</b></li><li class=\"d0\">The man who thinks himself wise from the pagan world must <b>divest himself</b></li><li class=\"d1\">become like <b>Paul, the apostles, Christ on the cross</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"He needs to <b>start over</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":313.71,"lv":3,"h":"Q: Is classical philosophy compatible with Christ?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: would Paul say classical philosophy is <b>incompatible</b> with the wisdom of Christ?</li><li class=\"d1\">later people thought them very compatible - \"there were both sides\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul is <b>not anti-reason</b> - he's <b>anti-paganism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">these beliefs are grounded in <b>pagan gods</b> = real entities, <b>demons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"He doesn't think they don't exist\" - <b>demonic spirits misleading people</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Socrates</b>, in the <i>Republic</i> and the <i>Apology</i>: being <b>possessed by a demon</b> as a good thing</li><li class=\"d1\">philosophers would categorize their knowledge as demonic - meaning <b>inspired by the gods, divine</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul doesn't argue on a <b>secular basis</b> (\"just your theory and ideas\")</li><li class=\"d1\">agrees the phenomenon comes from the <b>spiritual realm</b></li><li class=\"d1\">disagrees on <b>what kind of spirit</b> and how truthful</li><li class=\"d0\">Can't filter Plato into \"non-pagan stuff\" and \"pagan stuff\" - that's the <b>secular idea</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts 17</b>: quotes a hymn to <b>Zeus</b> - \"what have your own poets said?\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>bait and switch</b>, an evangelism tactic</li><li class=\"d1\">turns it on its head: the God this is true of <b>isn't Zeus</b>, it's the God I proclaim</li></ul>"},{"t":464.52,"lv":2,"h":"Ancient reason: the <i>nous</i> as a sense","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ancient understanding of reason <b>very different</b> from ours</li><li class=\"d1\">ours grounded in <b>math and laws of logic</b> - \"secular\", available to everyone</li><li class=\"d1\">ancients had no such concept</li><li class=\"d0\">Reason / wisdom = the <b><i>nous</i></b> - not just an Orthodox word</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Plato and Aristotle</b> use it; <b>Nicomachean Ethics</b>: highest of the rational virtues</li><li class=\"d0\">Intellect seen as a <b>sensory organ</b>, not a <b>\"computer in a box\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">thoughts <b>come into your mind from outside</b>; the mind perceives them \"like eyes\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>pagans, Jews, Christians</b> all believed this</li><li class=\"d1\">pagan in the woods with a partner: love/lust = <b>spirits in the woods</b> giving the thoughts</li><li class=\"d1\">modern fossil: <b>Cupid</b> with his arrow (hackneyed version)</li><li class=\"d0\">Fossil in language: <b>\"making sense\"</b> - reason as a sense</li><li class=\"d0\">Honing reason (Aristotle's virtue) = perceive these things <b>more correctly</b></li><li class=\"d0\">What we call reason = <b>calculation</b>, <i>logistikon</i> → <b>logistics</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"okay, we've had the big idea, now the little details\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>lesser thing</b>; real reason = perceiving <b>mentally</b></li></ul>"},{"t":664.66,"lv":3,"h":"The Jesus Prayer and the stream of thoughts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hard for a <b>modern materialist</b> to understand <b>contemplative prayer</b>, the <b>Jesus Prayer</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we think the stream of consciousness <b>is us</b>, \"the subject producing those\"</li><li class=\"d1\">ancient view: <b>not us</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Jesus Prayer = <b>prayer of a single thought</b></li><li class=\"d1\">take all the thoughts coming in, confusing us → <b>silence them by focusing on one</b></li><li class=\"d1\">extract yourself from the stream, <b>separate</b> from it</li><li class=\"d1\">holier people than me: silence even that one thought → <b>pure contemplation of God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why it matters: <b>examine your thoughts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">river image: pulled downstream, can't look at the banks, width, depth</li><li class=\"d1\">outside it: <b>where are these from? good or evil? get rid of / cling to?</b></li></ul>"},{"t":788.32,"lv":3,"h":"How Paul read Plato and the wisdom of this world","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul looking at Plato or a Platonist is <b>not</b> weighing their logic (\"point A, B, C, where do they go wrong?\")</li><li class=\"d1\">looking at how they <b>perceived the spiritual realm</b></li><li class=\"d1\">accurate perceptions → <b>use to proclaim the gospel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">inaccurate / lying → <b>counter</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Wisdom of this world\" = pagan wisdom</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no division between philosophical and religious</b> then</li><li class=\"d1\">Stoicism, Epicureanism, paganism (no word for it at the time)</li><li class=\"d1\">their <b>perceptions of the spiritual realm</b> - off base</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Misperception</b>: not imaginary or hallucinated → they were <b>lied to</b> by beings they interacted with</li><li class=\"d1\">later chapters (<b>pagan sacrifices</b>): affirms they <b>really interact with demons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christians must <b>not do that</b> - not truth, working toward <b>destruction</b>, chaos</li></ul>"},{"t":912.86,"lv":3,"h":"Oracles and Delphi; the Holy Spirit gives all truth","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Corinth's most \"spiritually advanced\": the <b>oracle</b> possessed, or one like <b>Socrates</b></li><li class=\"d1\">culture: closest to the gods → go to them for wisdom, problems, <b>the future</b> (<b>oracle at Delphi</b>)</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: <b>\"uh-uh\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the beings they communicate with <b>don't know anything</b> anyway (last time)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Christ is God, Holy Spirit is God</b> - Spirit in you connects you to Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ is the truth → <b>access to all truth</b></li></ul>"},{"t":969.78,"lv":3,"h":"Plato's and Paul's writings as spiritual experience","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Back to the original question: we see Plato's work as <b>product of his human mind</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even Christians talk about <b>St. Paul's mind</b> - he sat and thought about religion, then wrote</li><li class=\"d1\">not how <b>Paul or Plato</b> understood what they were doing</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's self-understanding: writing about <b>his own spiritual experience</b> - as real as physical</li><li class=\"d1\">writing what <b>Christ has taught him</b> (said in various letters)</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ appeared to him <b>at least three times in Acts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">relationship with Christ <b>literal</b>, \"not just some sense\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Socrates</b> / Plato: same - <b>Aristotle's <i>Metaphysics</i></b> = experience of being, \"almost prayerful\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So \"what would Paul think valid in Plato?\" - he wouldn't look that way</li><li class=\"d1\">valid = true? → \"reflects their experience, but experience was with <b>lying spirits</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Different view of <b>reason, truth, wisdom</b> → different results</li></ul>"},{"t":1103.67,"lv":3,"h":"Projecting ourselves onto the past; the Church's liturgy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Fundamental issue in interpreting scripture: <b>we project ourselves</b> into the past</li><li class=\"d1\">assume Paul's Christianity / religious experience was <b>like mine</b>, same worldview</li><li class=\"d1\">obviously false - 2,000 years, different culture - \"but we do it anyway\"</li><li class=\"d1\">we do it in how we read: <b>modern English</b>, other cultures and times</li><li class=\"d0\">Today we have plenty of info to <b>reconstruct the ancient worldview</b> - texts, translations</li><li class=\"d0\">But before that: <b>13th century Constantinople</b> didn't have those - they had <b>the liturgy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">liturgy still comes from <b>that perspective</b></li><li class=\"d1\">origin of <b>interpreting scripture in the Church</b> - maintained the ancient perspective and practices</li><li class=\"d1\">e.g. the <b>prayer practices</b> - kept the ancient sense of reason</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Important for everything we talk about in this Bible study\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1236.44,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 3:19-20 - The wise caught in craftiness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:19</b>: \"wisdom of this world is <b>foolishness with God</b>\" - \"He catches the wise in their own <b>craftiness</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:20</b>: \"the Lord knows the thoughts of the wise, that they are <b>futile</b>\" (Psalm)</li><li class=\"d0\">Who are the wise? <b>pagan gods</b> and the people with knowledge from them</li><li class=\"d1\">\"the <b>rulers of this world</b>, those gods themselves\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Craftiness</b> → the <b>serpent</b> in Genesis: \"more clever and subtle\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul doesn't firmly distinguish - true of both</li><li class=\"d1\">English \"craftiness\" has an air of <b>sneakiness</b> - spirits <b>manipulating us</b></li><li class=\"d0\">They think they're wise, clever - but God knows their thoughts: <b>futile = come to nothing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same as the <b>rulers of this world</b> \"coming to nothing\" - \"already over for them\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1326.26,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 3:21-23 - All things are yours","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:21</b>: \"let no one boast in men, for <b>all things are yours</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">already yours because you have the <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">just need to <b>come to maturity</b> in understanding</li><li class=\"d0\">Corinth is doing a bad job, but they <b>do have the Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul compared them to <b>babies</b> - baby is human, <b>all the DNA</b> is there</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>potency → actuality</b> - \"very Aristotelian\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but they're <b>still babies</b> with an infantile understanding</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:22-23</b>: \"Paul or Apollos or Cephas or the world or life or death or things present or to come - all are yours\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>you are Christ's, Christ is God's</b></li><li class=\"d1\">nothing they have to teach you</li></ul>"},{"t":1429.41,"lv":3,"h":"Source: new covenant writes the Torah on the heart","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Source: <b>Old Testament tradition</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jeremiah 31, Joel 2, Ezekiel</b> - new covenant: Spirit writes the <b>Torah on your heart</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no longer</b> will a man teach his brother to know the Lord - <b>everyone will</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: \"I'm <b>irrelevant</b> to boot\" - happened to be the one who came originally</li><li class=\"d1\">you have everything because you have the <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Spirit → belong to <b>Christ</b> → belong to the <b>Father</b> - <b>Trinitarian</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>All of God is in you, available</b> - you're <b>not using it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">because you're looking for wisdom in <b>Christian teachers, or worse pagan teachers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"they don't have anything to offer you\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1502.84,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 4:1-2 - Servants and stewards of the mysteries","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:1</b>: \"Let a man so consider us as <b>servants of Christ</b> and <b>stewards of the mysteries of God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">how you should look at <b>me, Peter, Apollos</b></li><li class=\"d1\">stewards in his house: \"we guide you in when you show up, <b>take your coat</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not what modern ears hear: \"we've got the mysteries, we'll pass them on\" - <b>exactly what he's not saying</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Context of Corinth: <b>mysteries = initiation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">trying to come into <b>contact with the gods, spirits directly</b></li><li class=\"d1\">people doing the ritual with you, <b>masks, dark</b> - you're not coming to see them</li><li class=\"d1\">here: coming to <b>God the Father through Christ</b>; \"we're the helpers\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox Church calls the <b>sacraments \"mysteries\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>baptism</b>: baptized into Christ, not Paul</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm the steward who <b>pushed you under the water</b>\" - \"a <b>functionary</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1629.66,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 4:2-4 - Faithful servants; judged by the Lord","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:2</b>: \"it is required in stewards that one be <b>found faithful</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">fan club problem: Paul's club vs Peter's → arguments about who's better</li><li class=\"d1\">answer: <b>we're all servants</b>; servant assessed by <b>faithfulness to his master</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:3</b>: \"a <b>very small thing</b> that I should be judged by you or by a human court\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I do not even judge myself\" - \"<b>I don't care</b>\" how you judge me</li><li class=\"d1\">fan or \"thinks I'm the worst apostle\" - \"Great\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:4</b>: \"I know of nothing against myself, yet I am <b>not justified</b> by this\"</li><li class=\"d1\">if he knew, he'd <b>repent</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>justification</b> (Romans) = who's on Christ's <b>right</b> not left</li><li class=\"d1\">not knowing any sin off the top of my head ≠ <b>righteous</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"he who judges me is the Lord\"</b> - Christ decides how faithful</li></ul>"},{"t":1745.64,"lv":3,"h":"4:5 - Judge nothing before the time","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:5</b>: \"judge nothing before the time until the Lord comes\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not your job to decide <b>good people and bad, righteous and sinners, good and lousy Christians</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Christ is coming</b> and will judge</li><li class=\"d0\">When he comes: <b>bring to light the hidden things of darkness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>spiritual evil</b> - exposes it and what it's been doing</li><li class=\"d1\">reveals the <b>counsels of the hearts</b> - what's really in your heart</li><li class=\"d1\">\"he's the one <b>actually qualified</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Each one's praise will come from <b>God</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1812.17,"lv":3,"h":"Praise, \"don't judge,\" and gossip","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul isn't saying \"don't judge\" because he's upset</li><li class=\"d1\">our modern \"don't judge me\" - from people <b>doing things they shouldn't</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: \"I <b>don't want your praise</b>\" - fans saying \"I'm of Paul\"</li><li class=\"d1\">praise is a <b>judgment, too</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Only praise he wants: from <b>God on judgment day</b>, for faithfulness</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you can all hate me for all I care\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Better if you <b>didn't judge at all</b> - less time forming opinions of others</li><li class=\"d1\">\"that would <b>kill gossip</b>\" - that's what gossip basically is</li></ul>"},{"t":1889.23,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 4:6 - Not beyond what is written","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:6</b>: \"figuratively transferred to myself and Apollos for your sakes\" - <b>learn in us not to think beyond what is written</b></li><li class=\"d1\">been talking about <b>me, Apollos, Peter</b> - applies to <b>all of you judging each other</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Purpose: <b>none puffed up</b> on behalf of one against another</li><li class=\"d0\">When you judge, it <b>always works out</b> in your favor</li><li class=\"d1\">rare to meet someone who thinks he's <b>worst of everybody</b></li><li class=\"d1\">rare we're sincere: \"Christ came to save sinners <b>of whom I am chief</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">usually a <b>\"cousin\"</b> in the back of the head - \"I'm not that bad\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Judging → find somebody we think we're <b>doing better than</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>puffed up, inflated</b> - pride, which will <b>destroy us</b></li></ul>"}]});
